\f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs117\super $}{ Print/Merge Command}}#{\footnote \pard\plain \s121\ql \li120\ri0\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin120\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\cs117\super #}{ cmd_printmerge}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s121\ql \li120\ri0\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin120\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs117\super K}{ Commands;Commands,Print/Merge;}}}{\fs20 The}{
\b0\fs20  }{\fs20 Print/Merge Command
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li120\ri0\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin120\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
This command allows a set of similar maps to be created automatically by merging a text file containing control information for each map with the current configuration and a macro file that controls the steps used to create each map.
\par To use this function you will need three things; First you need a configuration setup with the proper viewports, data, themes, infoboxes, etc to create the type of map you desire. Second you will need a text file which has the control information fo
r each map (this is referred to as the data file).  This file should be either comma or tab delimited with a header record containing the names of each field in the file.  Each record in the file will create one map.  The control information can be a UDI 
v
alue such as a PID, a reference number or coordinate data.  Other fields such as variable text to appear on each map can be included in the data file.  Thirdly you will need a macro file that controls the steps used to create each map.  This macro will be
 run once per map and would normally do things such as control the bounds for each viewport and determine what data is highlighted.
\par 
\par The following three examples should help clarify what the Print/Merge command can be used for:
\par }{\b Example 1:}{ Tax Forfeit Maps
\par }\pard\plain \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {A
 map is needed for each property on the tax forfeit list showing the size and dimensions of the property and the surrounding area within a quarter mile with parcels colored by assessed value.
\par }\pard \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sb120\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 {The following data file is used:
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li720\ri0\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {[SaleDate]=10/21/03;
\par [PropOffset]=}{\uldb $CONVERT}{\v df_convert}{(LENGTH,MILES,SYSTEM,0.25);
\par PID,HOUSE,STREET,CITY
\par 2711922340061,7021,Crest Drive,Maple Grove
\par 2102821220121,1104,46}{\super th}{ Street S.,Minneapolis
\par }\pard\plain \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sb120\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {The following macro file is used:
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li720\ri0\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {[BOUNDS]=}{\uldb $BOUNDS}{\v  df_bounds}{(TAG,PINCA:[PID]);
\par [BOUNDS]=}{\uldb $BOUNDS}{\v  df_bounds}{(INC, [PropOffset]);
\par [%C]=$HLT(ITEM,PINCA:[PID]);
\par }{\uldb $ZOOM}{\v df_zoom}{(BOUNDS,[BOUNDS],F,Primary Viewport);
\par }{\uldb $RETURN}{\v df_return}{(1);
\par }\pard\plain \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sb120\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
The configuration is set up to display parcel areas, lot dimensions and other desired data and contains an infobox containing 
the variables [HOUSE], [STREET] and [CITY]. It also contains a theme that colors the surrounding parcels based on assessed value, using a property valuation database for the entire city.
\par Note that the data file has two lines that come before the field name line.  A print/merge data file can have as many lines at the top as needed to set other global variables needed for the particular set of maps.  These lines must end in a semi-colon.

\par }\pard\plain \ql \li120\ri0\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin120\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b 
\par Example 2:}{ Garbage Route Maps
\par }\pard\plain \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {A map is needed to display the pickup sit
es for each garbage route for each day.  A DBF file is supplied which contains the parcel id (PID) for each collection site along with the route number and day of pickup.
\par }\pard \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sb120\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 {The following data file is used:
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li720\ri0\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {[MapDate]=10/21/03;
\par ROUTEID,PICKUPDAY
\par A01,2
\par A01,3,
\par A02,2
\par A02,3
\par }\pard\plain \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sb120\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {The following macro file is used:
\par }\pard \s123\ql \li720\ri0\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin720\itap0 {[MAPBOUNDS]=}{\uldb $BOUNDS}{\v  df_bounds}{(INIT);
\par [%C]=}{\uldb $OPEN}{\v  df_open}{(PARCLIST=[%DL]attribut\\garrout.dbf,ROUTE='[ROUTEID] AND DAY=[PICKUPDAY]');
\par WHILE(}{\uldb $FETCH}{\v  df_fetch}{(PARCLIST))\{
\par \tab [PARBOUNDS]=}{\uldb $BOUNDS}{\v  df_bounds}{(TAG,PINCA:[PID]);
\par \tab IF(!}{\uldb $BOUNDS}{\v  df_bounds}{(VALID,[PARBOUNDS]))
\par \tab \{[%C]=}{\uldb $APPEND}{\v  df_append}{(c:\\garberrs.txt,[MAPTITLE],[PID]);\}
\par \tab ELSE
\par \tab \{[MAPBOUNDS]=}{\uldb $BOUNDS}{\v  df_bounds}{(ADD,[MAPBOUNDS],[PARBOUNDS]);\};
\par \};
\par [%C]=}{\uldb $CLOSE}{\v  df_close}{(PARCLIST);
\par $RETURN(}{\uldb $ZOOM}{\v  df_zoom}{(BOUNDS,[MAPBOUNDS],T,Primary Viewport));
\par }\pard \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sb120\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 {
The configuration is set up to display parcel areas with address, street names and other desired data and contains an infobox containing the variables [ROUTEID] and $DOWNAME([PICKUPDAY])
\par In this case the print/merge data file is just a l
ist of all the route/day combinations.  The macro file retrieves the parcels for each map and creates an overall bounds for the map by combining the bounds for each parcel.  If a parcel in the DBF file is not found in the map its PID is written to the err
or file c:\\garberrs.txt.
\par The configuration file also contains a theme, which colors those parcels which match the day and route for a particular map and skip those which do not.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li120\ri0\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin120\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\b Example 3:}{ Plotting Highlighted Items
\par }\pard\plain \s123\ql \li360\ri0\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin360\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {This example shows how to set up a genera
l-purpose print/merge that can be used to create a separate plot for each item in the highlight list.  Items can be placed in the highlight list in a variety of ways, from picking them individually with the mouse to selecting a class of items from a theme
.  Once the list has been created, this print/merge will create a separate plot for each item using the current configuration and optionally will create an index map showing the bounds of each individual map displayed on a city-wide base map.
\par }}